![HAMCover](/assets/images/HAM-Cover.jpeg){: .align-center width="600px"}

The colder months are upon us. I, for one, am not a huge fan of the weather, as I have always enjoyed the beauty of the transitional seasons the most. Spring and fall are when I thrive, when it’s not too cold to have some fun outside but not so hot that I melt. Additionally, these seasons are the most beautiful here in New England. The foliage takes on new forms and colors, creating the perfect atmosphere. However, that all ends at some point. We’re hitting the time in November when everything goes from vibrant oranges and reds to bare trees and gray skies. Of course, there is a silver lining. When it starts to get too cold for my taste, I am never one to complain about holing up inside and enjoying my hobbies. I have already started to think about some of my personal hobby goals for the winter, including painting more Warhammer 40K models, finally finishing some Magic: The Gathering decks that have been waiting for me for quite some time now, and of course playing as many board games as humanly possible. With that in mind, it’s time for another board game review! This month, I had a little bit of trouble picking a game that would fit the vibe of the season. Believe it or not, there are not too many Thanksgiving-themed board games. However, after watching my family catch the same cold from one another in the span of a week, I knew exactly what game to play.

Pandemic by Z-Man Games is one of my favorite games in my collection and one that I have the oldest memories of. I was first introduced to the game in the fifth grade during the first session of my school’s games club. At the time, a board game where the table works collectively to overcome some obstacle was completely foreign to me. After playing with some other students and faculty, I ran home and begged my mom to get a copy of my own. After I received the game the following Christmas, it grew into a family game night staple. It’s been some time since I picked up the game, as I have been exploring so many other titles in the meantime, so I’m thrilled to pull it off the shelf and play with such great memories in mind. Last weekend, my partner and I sat down to play the game. Like the last time we did a review, we aimed to play the game a number of times. For the first few, we hoped to play just the two of us. Afterward, we aimed to play with a few more people to see if that would change our perspectives. After reaching out to a few people to see who was available, tragedy struck… Everyone was busy. A true board game nerd’s nightmare. This meant that my partner and I would have to brave the pandemic on our own. Opening the box and setting up the game, I was hit with a wave of nostalgia. Before this game, I was only familiar with the classic board games that were readily available in every household. After I played this for the first time, my eyes were opened to all the cool possibilities of games and the amazing stories they could tell.

![HAMMonsters](/assets/images/HAM-Monsters.jpeg){: .align-center width="600px"}

To get things kicked off, let’s start with the concept. At first glance, the idea behind this game seems super new and unique. As far as I know, Pandemic is in a league of its own when it comes to games about fighting off disease. However, the number of spin-off games that this title has fostered sullies the concept a little in my mind. I will say, after taking a moment to read some of the descriptions of the spin-off titles, some of them seem cooler than they did at first glance and might be worth checking out. That said, I feel like I cannot take points away from it for that as I have not played any of the other games. This results in a concept score of 4. The concept is super exciting, but in order to get a 5, I would have to be absolutely blown away.

Setting up the game was no problem at all. My partner and I read over the rules and were very happy with how simple they were. You get four actions per turn, using each to do one of eight things, four of which are movement. Picking up and playing this game is no challenge at all. Even though the mechanics of the game were simple enough to understand after a brief read over the instructions, I would not say that this made the game any less interesting. In my book, this gives the clarity rating a 5 out of 5. Absolutely perfect. I wouldn’t change a thing.

As we played, we both enjoyed the tension and pressure that the game delivered. We went from city to city curing its citizens of infection. Eventually, we pulled the wrong card and just like that, the game was over. At that point, we had been playing for a total of 10 minutes. We were both a little upset that the game ended with just a flip of a card, so we decided to set up again and give it another shot. In round two, we moved about the same way as before, saving cities and discovering cures. But once again, one card proved to be the end of our run. At this point, we were definitely noticing the pattern of an anticlimactic loss. Third time’s a charm, so we set the game up and pushed on. This time, we paid better attention to the immediate threats and dealt with the conflicts as they arose. As soon as we had the resources to discover the third and final cure, we walked into the research center and won. Just like that. Nothing flashy or tense. We were visibly disappointed at that point. We both appreciated the win, but it felt more lackluster than it should have. I was willing to excuse this to some degree and give the design a 4, but my partner insisted that this was worthy of a 3. So design gets a 3 out of 5.

After packing up the game, we took a retrospective look at the replayability of the game. Sure, you can play as different characters and increase the difficulty, but other than that, the game is essentially the same. Once you get the rhythm down and get a little bit lucky, you can find yourself coming out on top more often than not. Compared to other games such as Horrified and Betrayal at the House on the Hill, the replayability just isn’t there. Because of this, I think it is fair to say that it deserves a 3 out of 5. It is what you would expect from a modern game.

Wrapping it all up, I’m giving Pandemic a total score of 3 out of 5. While it will always be a nostalgic game for me that led me into the wonderful world of board games, I think I played through it with rose-tinted glasses on. Thankfully, my partner pulled me back down to earth and pointed out some of the things wrong with the game. In hindsight, I totally agree as well. Nothing makes me more disappointed than an anticlimactic game. I would be curious to see if this problem has been resolved in any of their other titles, which I will be sure to check out. That said, this does not mean this was a bad game at all. This will always be one of my favorite games to play with people who are not used to more complex games. Not to mention, the stress and pressure that it can put on you at times will always be such a cool feeling. If you have not picked this game up yet, I would seriously recommend it for your game shelf.

Now that the cold months are coming in quickly, I hope you all find time to see loved ones and enjoy all your favorite indoor activities. I, for one, am looking forward to all the books I will read and Magic: The Gathering decks I can build in the meantime before I come out of hibernation. Have a nice November and find a cool board game to play. Now if you’ll excuse me, I have a scary amount of Warhammer to paint. See ya!
